WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.920
J. R. R. Tolkien's "The Lord of the Rings"&nbsp;
trilogy is one of the most important works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.660
of fantasy ever written, and is a touchstone&nbsp;
upon which many of today's fantasy worlds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.100
creatures are based. The world of Middle-earth&nbsp;
gained even more popularity when Peter Jackson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.800
adapted "The Lord of the Rings" into a film series&nbsp;
that managed to capture its unique appeal, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.180
settings and characters that Tolkien created&nbsp;
have continued finding new fans to this day.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.320
Much of the appeal of "The Lord of the Rings"&nbsp;
comes from the impressive cast of characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.320
that Tolkien conjured up for his timeless fantasy&nbsp;
saga. At the core of these is Frodo Baggins, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.380
Samwise Gamgee, his steadfast and loyal friend.&nbsp;
Sam begins the story as a risk-averse gardener…

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.720
"If I take one more step, it'll be the&nbsp;
farthest away from home I've ever been."

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.080
… but by the end of the tale,&nbsp;
he's been transformed into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.920
legendary hero on the same level as&nbsp;
any among the Fellowship of the Ring.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.980
It's perhaps for this reason that J.R.R. Tolkien&nbsp;
considered Samwise Gamgee to be the true hero of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.200
"The Lord of the Rings", remarking in a letter&nbsp;
to potential publisher Milton Waldman in 1951,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.160
"I think the simple 'rustic' love of&nbsp;
Sam and his Rosie [...] is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.220
essential to the study of his&nbsp;
(the chief hero's) character…"

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.860
Tolkien's assertion that Sam is the "chief hero"&nbsp;
is probably no surprise to Lord of the Rings fans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.560
Though Frodo Baggins takes on the task of bearing&nbsp;
the One Ring to Mount Doom, he begins to falter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.180
over the course of his long journey, eventually&nbsp;
deciding to take the ring for himself when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.400
finally comes time to destroy it. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
Sam's time with the ring sees him single-mindedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.260
devoted to saving Frodo, and he never even&nbsp;
considers keeping the cursed object for himself.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.680
Sam overcomes his humble beginnings to defeat&nbsp;
a giant spider. He also slays a tower full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.420
Orcs to rescue Frodo and is so formidable&nbsp;
an opponent that the Orcs believe him to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:54.300
a legendary Elven warrior. Finally, when Frodo&nbsp;
can no longer walk, it's Sam who carries him up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.800
Mount Doom so that he can complete his task.&nbsp;
Still, there's even more to Sam than this.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:07.200
"Samwise the Brave. I want to hear more about&nbsp;
Sam. Frodo wouldn't have got far without Sam."

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.540
J.R.R. Tolkien had some real-life heroes in&nbsp;
mind when he created the character of Sam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:17.400
as reflected in a 1956 letter to&nbsp;
editor H. Cotton Minchin, which read,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.200
“My ‘Samwise’ is indeed (as you note)&nbsp;
largely a reflexion of the English soldier…"

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.240
Notably, Tolkien served in the British&nbsp;
military during World War I and as such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.820
witnessed great acts of heroism firsthand.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.900
The author admired these soldiers so&nbsp;
deeply that he wrote that they were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.820
quote, "so far superior to [him]." When a&nbsp;
man with the same name as the heroic Hobbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:44.100
wrote to Tolkein in 1956, Tolkien said that it&nbsp;
was an honor to have that name, writing back,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.640
"The 'Sam Gamgee' of my story is a most heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.980
character [...] even though&nbsp;
his origins are rustic."

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.760
With these words in mind, it's&nbsp;
clear that Tolkien admired Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.540
just as much as readers and viewers&nbsp;
have come to for generations since.

